 "lesson": {
  "slides": [
   {
    "id": "L1",
    "type": "mc",
    "text": "**FTC, part 2:** if $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$, then $int[a,b] f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)$. No more rectangles! For $int[0,2] x^2 dx$, which $F$ can you use?",
    "options": [
     "$F(x) = frac{x^3}{3}$",
     "$F(x) = 2x$",
     "$F(x) = x^2$",
     "$F(x) = frac{x^2}{2}$"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "hint": "We need $F' = x^2$.",
    "explain": "$frac{x^3}{3}$ (or $frac{x^3}{3} + C$; the $C$ cancels)."
   },
   {
    "id": "L2",
    "type": "num",
    "text": "So $int[0,2] x^2 dx = F(2) - F(0) = frac{8}{3} - 0$. Enter it.",
    "answer": 2.666667,
    "label": "integral =",
    "hint": "Use the fraction key, or 2.667.",
    "explain": "$frac{8}{3}$, exactly what the rectangles were closing in on."
   },
   {
    "id": "L3",
    "type": "expr",
    "text": "**FTC, part 1:** $frac{d}{dx} int[a,x] f(t) dt = f(x)$. The derivative of the area function is the function. Find $frac{d}{dx} int[1,x] t^3 dt$.",
    "answer": "x^3",
    "vars": [
     "x"
    ],
    "keys": [
